CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ constexpr member fn template as constant: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ sz<char> == 1: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ sz<int> distinct: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ runtime: distinct bodies: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG sz<int>==1 \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.inst]/5 + [expr.const]: a call to a constexpr static member function
template with explicit template arguments must be usable as a constant
expression, which requires implicitly instantiating the specialization's
definition.  CBMC previously left the member function template uninstantiated
(its body still referenced the template parameter), so the call could not be
folded; distinct specializations also collided on a single unsuffixed member
symbol (unsound).  Assertions 1-4 prove the call folds and that distinct
specializations are independent entities with correct values; assertion 5 (a
wrong value) must FAIL, proving non-vacuity.
